Analysis

    ① 2026-06-05 Decline Triggers (confirmed):
       - Direct cause: Broadcom Q2 accounting results missed + AI chip Q3
         guidance of $16B announced below expectations
       - Shock transmission: NVDA -6%, Micron -4%, Qualcomm/Intel/AMD -3%+,
         Samsung Electronics -6.4%, SK Hynix -9%+
       - ~$923B wiped from top-10 semiconductor market cap (NVDA alone $280B)
       - KOSPI intraday -6%+, heavy foreign selling, partial sidecar circuit
         reported
       - Secondary trigger: NVDA Rubin platform SOCAMM memory downgrade rumor
         (NVL72 rack memory: 55TB → 28TB reduction claim) — unconfirmed by NVDA
         ※ unconfirmed

    ② SOXL "second-largest historical decline" claim (※ needs verification):
       - Fact: SOXL closing price 2026-06-05 $191.44 (vs. previous $262.70,
         approx. -27%)
       - Additional fact: SOXL -20.9% single-day decline also reported on
         2026-05-12
       - "Second-largest in history" exact ranking cannot be directly verified —
         but the COVID-shock-level volatility is confirmed as fact. With a 3×
         leveraged ETF, concern about permanent NAV impairment (volatility decay)
         is valid.

    ③ Conspiracy Theory ① US Government Buying OpenAI — False or Unconfirmed:
       - WebSearch found no official policy confirmation.
       - What was confirmed: Sam Altman proposed "collective ownership of AI"
         (the public becomes shareholders in the AI industry) as an alternative
         to UBI. Different structure from government buying.
       - Conclusion: "artificial price suppression" hypothesis cannot be adopted
         as an investment thesis. The 6/5 decline is explained by the clear
         fundamental trigger of Broadcom's guidance.
       - Paradoxical implication: the fact that the conspiracy is false
         strengthens the bearish case — it means this is not irrational panic
         but a normal bear entry driven by consensus downgrade.

    ④ Conspiracy Theory ② SpaceX IPO Cash Drain — Circumstantially Confirmed:
       - SpaceX IPO targeting 2026-06-12, raising $75B (556.6M shares @ $135),
         largest IPO in history ($1.75T valuation).
       - JPMorgan estimate: $2T valuation + 50% free float → passive funds
         would need to sell ~$950B of major tech stocks to rebalance —
         explicit "siphon effect."
       - Conclusion: a fundamentals-unrelated structural capital outflow trigger
         is real. The 6/5 plunge can be interpreted as the first wave of
         front-running. With 2nd-wave supply post-6/12 and lock-up/secondary
         overhang, short-term bounces risk being dead-cat bounces.

    ⑤ Fundamentals vs. Supply/Demand Collision:
       - HBM cycle peak is assessed at H2 2026 — Samsung Q2 prelim 7/8,
         Samsung formal 7/23, SK Hynix 7/29, NVDA 8/26 earnings pipeline
         imminent.
       - Cumulative gains of SK Hynix +169% / Samsung +96% represent a premium
         atop the "HBM supercycle in perpetuity" assumption. Broadcom guidance +
         Rubin memory downgrade rumor are the first signals that shake that
         assumption.

    ⑥ FX effect: USD/KRW 1,558.84 — foreign-selling-driven KRW weakness
       paradoxically supports KR exporters' next-quarter earnings. Samsung Q2
       preliminary earnings surprise potential remains intact (7/8).

